W(SC,"Explain XSS types and how to prevent them.",[["stored","reflected","dom"],["escape","encode","sanitiz"],["csp","content security policy"],["innerhtml","textcontent","framework"],["httponly","cookie"]],"Stored (persisted), reflected (echoed from the request) and DOM-based (client JS writes untrusted data to a sink). Prevent via contextual output encoding, avoiding dangerous sinks (innerHTML), sanitising allowed HTML, strict CSP with nonces, framework auto-escaping and HttpOnly cookies to limit impact.");
W(SC,"CSRF vs XSS: how do they differ and how do you defend against each?",[["csrf","forged","cross-site request","logged-in"],["xss","script injection","inject"],["samesite","token","origin check"],["csp","escape","sanitize","encode"]],"CSRF abuses the browser's automatic credential sending to perform actions; defend with SameSite cookies, anti-CSRF tokens and Origin checks. XSS runs attacker script in your origin (which can defeat CSRF defences); defend with encoding, sanitisation and CSP.");
W(SC,"Where should you store auth tokens in a web app and why?",[["httponly","cookie"],["samesite","secure","csrf"],["localstorage","xss","memory"],["refresh","short-lived","rotation","access token"]],"Prefer HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookies so scripts can't read them, plus CSRF protection. If tokens must be handled in JS, keep short-lived access tokens in memory and rotate refresh tokens. localStorage exposes tokens to any XSS.");
W(SC,"How do you secure third-party scripts on a page?",[["csp","allowlist"],["sri","integrity"],["sandbox","iframe"],["audit","minimi","self-host","tag manager","review"]],"Limit them (audit necessity), self-host or pin versions, add SRI hashes, restrict via CSP, sandbox in iframes where possible, load them async/deferred and monitor for changes, as third-party scripts can read the page and exfiltrate data.");

