W(TE,"How would you test a React component that fetches data?",[["testing library","render","getbyrole","findby"],["msw","mock","network","fetch"],["loading","error","success","states"],["userevent","interaction"],["waitfor","async","findby"]],"Render it with Testing Library, mock the network at the boundary (MSW), assert loading, success and error states using role-based async queries (findBy/waitFor), and simulate interaction with user-event. Avoid testing implementation details.");
W(TE,"What is your testing strategy for a large front-end application?",[["unit","pure"],["integration","component"],["e2e","playwright","cypress","critical"],["ci","parallel","flaky"],["accessib","visual","contract","type"]],"Mix: static checks (TypeScript/ESLint), unit tests for logic, integration/component tests for most behaviour, a small set of e2e tests for critical user journeys, plus accessibility, visual regression and contract tests where valuable. Run in CI, quarantine flaky tests and keep suites fast.");
W(TE,"When is mocking harmful?",[["implementation","coupled","brittle"],["real","behaviour","integration","false confidence"],["boundary","network","external"]],"Over-mocking couples tests to implementation, so refactors break tests while real bugs slip through, giving false confidence. Mock at system boundaries (network, time, randomness), not your own modules.");

